o, any additional reliability or health data for A and D that could shift the balance.\n**What would change my vote:** Strong evidence that B\u2019s interpersonal behavior leads to crew conflict or safety\u2011critical miscommunication, or compelling evidence that C\u2019s communication difficulties are minor while his crew\u2011focused attitude and decision speed are robust, would make me favor C instead.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_3\", \"vote\": \"Candidate B\"}", "thoughts": "We have info from agent1 about teamwork etc. Now we need to consider other attributes we have: from initial list.\n\nLet's list all attributes per candidate:\n\nA: can anticipate dangerous situations. able to see complex connections. excellent spatial vision. very good leadership qualities. Unfriendly. eats unhealthily.\n\nB: very conscientious. handles stress very well. good at assessing weather conditions. excellent computer skills. regarded as pretentious. sometimes adopts wrong tone when communicating.\n\nC: can make correct decisions quickly. difficulty communicating ideas. regarded as egocentric. not very willing to further his education. puts concern for others above everything. excellent attention skills.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately. can concentrate very well. solves problems extremely well. takes responsibility seriously. considered moody. regarded as a loner.\n\nNow we need to evaluate for long-distance pilot: reliability, technical competence, calmness, decision-making, teamwork, professional development.\n\nWe need to weigh strengths and weaknesses.\n\nCandidate A: strong technical (spatial vision, complex connections, anticipate danger) good leadership, but unfriendly, unorganized, unhealthy (maybe reliability). Unfriendly may affect crew. Unhealthy could affect long flights. No mention of stress handling.\n\nCandidate B: conscientious, handles stress well, good weather assessment, computer skills (important for avionics). But pretentious, wrong tone